<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Mahabad</h1><p class="st-meta">1946 CE – 1946 CE · capital Mahabad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The short-lived Kurdish republic of 1946, the first modern Kurdish state, whose leaders' fate shaped Kurdish nationalism for generations.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>During the Soviet occupation of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, Kurdish nationalists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a> formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-for-the-revival-of-kurdistan">Komala</a> society and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi-muhammad">Qazi Muhammad</a>, a respected judge and religious leader. With Soviet backing, the Kurdish Republic was proclaimed in Mahabad on 22 January 1946.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic controlled a small area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a>. It made Kurdish the language of schools and government, published newspapers and journals, and created a small army whose most important commander was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-barzani">Mustafa Barzani</a>, leader of Iraqi Kurdish fighters who had taken refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. It cooperated with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/azerbaijan-peoples-government">Azerbaijan People's Government</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> while disputing border districts with it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Soviet withdrawal, Iranian troops entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a> in December 1946. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi-muhammad">Qazi Muhammad</a> was hanged in March 1947; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-barzani">Barzani</a> led his men on a march to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, later returning to lead the Kurdish movement in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-iraq">Republic of Iraq</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-mahabad.jpg" alt="Republic of Mahabad" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: محک, Zt-freak, Thespoondragon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_Mahabad.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1946 CE – 1946 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mahabad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Qazi Muhammad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kurdish (Sorani)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iran</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kurdish Republic of Mahabad, Republic of Kurdistan</dd></dl></aside></div>
